{
  "summary": "This paper introduces GraphAny, a novel model for fully-inductive node classification on arbitrary graphs. The model operates in a setting where both the graph structure and feature/label spaces differ between training and test graphs. GraphAny combines LinearGNNs (non-parametric models with analytical solutions) and an inductive attention module to achieve generalization across diverse graphs. The attention module is designed to be permutation-invariant and dimensionally robust, using entropy-normalized distance features. Empirically, GraphAny achieves strong performance on a diverse set of 31 datasets, outperforming both inductive and transductive baselines.",
  "strengths": [
    "Introduces a novel and practical problem setting (fully-inductive node classification) that generalizes beyond prior work.",
    "Proposes an elegant and theoretically grounded model (GraphAny) that combines analytical and learned components.",
    "The entropy normalization and attention module are novel and address critical challenges in dimensional robustness and permutation invariance.",
    "Comprehensive experiments on 31 diverse datasets with clear comparisons to both inductive and transductive baselines.",
    "Demonstrates significant performance improvements, even surpassing transductive models trained on each test dataset individually."
  ],
  "weaknesses": [
    {
      "issue": "The paper does not provide detailed comparisons to other non-parametric methods (e.g., label propagation) in the fully-inductive setting, despite mentioning them in the baseline section.",
      "location": "section/4.1",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "issue": "The reproducibility of experiments is not clearly described, and the code and data are not included in the submission. This makes it difficult to verify the results independently.",
      "location": "section/4",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "issue": "The paper assumes the pseudo-inverse $F_L^+$ is always well-defined, but no discussion is provided about the conditions under which this is valid (e.g., rank of $F_L$).",
      "location": "section/3.1",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "issue": "The paper claims GraphAny is 'the first' fully-inductive node classification model, but it does not thoroughly discuss prior work that may have overlapping ideas (e.g., training-free GNNs or graph foundation models).",
      "location": "section/2",
      "severity": "major"
    }
  ],
